{"id": 28, "ts": 1780424564.0099823, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I remain inclined to recommend **Candidate\u202fB** as the best overall fit.\u202fJames highlighted B\u2019s strong operational reliability (\u201cvery conscientious\u201d), excellent stress resilience (\u201chandles stress very well\u201d), and solid technical competence (weather assessment and computer skills).\u202fThese are core for long\u2011distance operations where consistent performance under pressure and accurate system handling are non\u2011negotiable.\nI do share the concerns raised about B\u2019s interpersonal style\u2014James noted \u201cnasty remarks about his colleagues,\u201d and my own notes add that he is \u201cregarded as pretentious\u201d and \u201csometimes adopts the wrong tone when communicating.\u201d\u202fThese issues could undermine crew cooperation and professional communication, which are essential in the cockpit.\nWhen I compare B to **Candidate\u202fC**, Sarah\u2019s assessment shows C\u2019s 100\u202f% reliability and rapid correct decision\u2011making, plus a positive crew atmosphere.\u202fHowever, C also \u201chas difficulty communicating ideas,\u201d is \u201cregarded as egocentric,\u201d and \u201cis not very willing to further his education,\u201d all of which raise serious doubts about his long\u2011term adaptability and teamwork.\nGiven the current evidence, B\u2019s technical and stress\u2011handling strengths outweigh his communication flaws, especially since those can be addressed through targeted training and coaching.\u202fC\u2019s communication and learning gaps are more entrenched and harder to remediate.\u202fTherefore, I continue to vote for Candidate\u202fB, while recommending that we plan a focused behavioral interview to verify his willingness to improve his interpersonal conduct before final selection."}
